WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.360
- [Reporter] Each trading
day at 11:00 a.m.,

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:05.670
the Chicago Mercantile Exchange, or CME,

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.750
host 10 minutes of
electronic trading of these,

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.060
blocks of wholesale America-
made cheddar cheese.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.830
Like some other food products,

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.820
the last couple of
years of cheddar trading

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:17.920
have seen some wild sessions.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.870
For example, here's a drop
that analysts attribute

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.190
to the first coronavirus lockdowns,

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.120
when schools and
restaurants started closing

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:26.760
and halting purchases.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.990
But during these swings on the CME,

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:30.380
the price of cheese here,

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:33.020
at the supermarket, held pretty stable.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.250
In fact, if you chart it out,

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.510
the price consumers pay for
cheese has stayed more steady

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:40.430
than some other common foods.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:44.560
Here's the chains for ground
beef and eggs and apples,

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.080
and here's cheese.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.890
There has been some inflation,

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:51.370
but the swings you see on
the CME are largely absent.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:54.290
With the price of many
foods on the rise right now,

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:57.080
we wanted to better understand why,

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.410
even with the choppy wholesale prices,

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:01.580
has the price of much of the cheese we buy

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.703
at the store stayed pretty stable?

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:08.260
The wild swings in wholesale
cheese prices play out

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.290
on the CME's auction platform.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:13.100
Traders buy and sell those 40-pound blocks

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.860
and 500-pound barrels of cheddar.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:17.390
The blocks are sliced and shredded

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:20.000
and sold to grocery stores,
restaurants, and others.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.760
And the barrels are primarily turned

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.520
into processed cheese products.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.710
These are the only cheese
traded on commodity markets

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.600
and cheese sellers all over
the country watch the prices,

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.000
especially of the blocks.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.583
- Okay, so I make Swiss cheese.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.590
There's no stock market for Swiss cheese,

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:38.530
there's no futures
market for Swiss cheese,

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:43.300
but can I use the insight
I got on cheddar pricing

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.530
to say something about
what Swiss is worth?

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.180
- [Reporter] To better
understand the wholesale swings,

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:53.170
we spoke to Andrew Novakovic,
an agricultural economist

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:56.300
who's been studying these
markets for over 40 years.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.730
- If I think, for example,

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:00.920
Swiss should always be
worth more than cheddar,

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.730
at least a little bit, if
the price of cheddar goes up,

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:06.970
well, maybe the price of Swiss
that I charge should go up.

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:08.900
- [Reporter] So when the
price of cheddar swings

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:10.640
like it did in 2020,

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.610
so does the wholesale
price of other cheeses.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:17.240
Again, here's where bulk orders
dried up early last year.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:18.410
And around here,

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.450
the government started
the Farmers to Family

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:23.460
food box program to help
farmers weather the storm.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.500
It included a promise to
buy a $100 million a month

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.993
in dairy products, including cheese.

00:02:30.878 --> 00:02:33.320
- Thank you. Have a good day.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:36.250
- There were several
different types of baskets

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:37.660
that they authorized.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:40.580
There was a dairy basket
and a milk basket.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.260
And they paid to acquire those products,

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.560
put them in a paper
bag or a cardboard box,

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.720
give them to local food
distribution centers.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.570
We saw the price of
cheese absolutely explode

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:56.110
for a short period of time

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.620
because there was this requirement

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.370
to get this huge volume of
cheese in a very narrow window.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:04.420
- [Reporter] After a few months of shocks,

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.830
prices returned to a bit
lower than what they were

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.600
right before the pandemic.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.730
As these programs ran their course,

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.810
influencing more sharp swings,

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.840
customers were picking up vast quantities

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.560
of cheese at stores,

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.180
which brings us to the question at hand.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:22.330
Why on average are these
swings hard to notice

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:23.670
in the dairy aisle?

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.240
Novakovic attributes it in part

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.190
to something called price smoothing.

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:31.910
- When prices are increasing
rapidly for some reason,

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:33.800
grocery stores tend to say

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.700
we don't want to reflect all of that.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.230
They anticipate being
able to recoup that margin

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.880
at some point when prices recede.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.960
So instead of seeing quite
the same rollercoaster

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:47.300
at the retail level,

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:52.133
generally consumers see
a much smoother effect.

00:03:52.133 --> 00:03:54.650
- [Reporter] Novakavic says
says customer expectations

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.670
can also influence pricing strategies.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:58.980
Take apples.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.810
Because they're grown seasonally,

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:02.850
customers may expect
them to go up and down

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.710
throughout the year more
than something like cheese.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:07.690
Also cheese is perishable.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:10.720
Customers may buy it more
frequently than other foods

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.130
and would be more likely
to notice price changes,

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:16.350
another incentive for groceries
to keep prices stable.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:17.860
While some in the cheese business

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.170
have had to weather big market swings,

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:22.340
a smaller section of
the industry has managed

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.310
to bypass much of the storm.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:27.440
Eran Wajswol, who owns
an artisanal creamery,

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.460
told us that while the
CME was swinging in 2020,

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.350
his business was thriving.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:35.240
- 2020 was one of our best years

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.920
because of farm markets and direct sales.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.230
The public do not want
to go to retail stores.

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:41.380
- [Reporter] Producing their own milk

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:44.170
and selling direct to
consumers allows some artisans

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:46.400
to skip the wild wholesale markets.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:49.700
- We are basically a farmstead creamery,

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.890
which means we make most
of our own milk onsite.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:57.620
We age our cheeses in a cave
onsite and we're original.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:58.900
- Wajs said that while he's protected

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:00.800
from some market conditions,

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.360
2021 has still presented challenges.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.160
- '21 is rough because of labor.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.180
We're producing and
selling everything we make,

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:11.100
but a few of us are working longer hours

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.320
to make the same stuff

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.990
because we can't find
enough people to make it.

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:16.890
- Wajs said that while
he's managed to hold steady

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.450
on prices so far,

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.580
he's feeling this year's
supply chain pressures

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:22.310
like much of the rest of the economy.

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:26.270
- The way we are doing it is
sometime increasing the minimum

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.020
that the customer has to buy from us,

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.300
changing delivery minimum,
increasing delivery prices,

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:32.260
trying to hold...

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.110
I don't know how long we can hold on.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:38.200
If it continues the way it's continuing,

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:41.020
it's gonna have to get a boost,

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:44.976
but we don't like to increase prices,

00:05:44.976 --> 00:05:49.570
and we haven't probably in
cheese for almost five years.

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:51.680
- [Reporter] As the economy
continues to see supply

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.400
and production challenges,

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.100
the price of cheese on commodity markets

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.811
and in the store remains uncertain.

00:05:58.811 --> 00:06:01.978
(quiet pensive music)

